## Implementing an Esolang
<hr class="section-rule lvl-2">

!!! tip "Tip"
    This demo builds on several others: an awk [polyglot]({{_}}/demos/polyglots) for the lexer, the [`io.stack.*` structured stack]({{_}}/standard-lib) for state, and the idea that [everything is a target]({{_}}/cmk/paradigm/#homoiconic), so a decoded program is just a stream of `make` goals.

Underload[^1] is a small, stack-based, concatenative esoteric language: a program is a string of single-character commands operating on a stack of strings.

**Command set for Underload**

| Operator | Operation | Effect |
| :-: | --- | --- |
| `(`...`)` | push | push the enclosed text onto the stack as a quotation |
| `~` | swap | `(x)(y)` -> `(y)(x)` |
| `:` | dup | `(x)` -> `(x)(x)` |
| `!` | discard | `(x)` -> (nothing) |
| `*` | concatenate | `(x)(y)` -> `(xy)` |
| `a` | enclose | `(x)` -> `((x))` |
| `S` | print | pop `(x)` and output `x` |
| `^` | apply | pop `(x)` and *run `x` as Underload* |

That last command (`^`) is what makes Underload Turing-complete, and makes quines delightfully easy.

<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

This demo implements a complete Underload interpreter with `compose.mk`, and the pieces map cleanly onto the standard library.

!!! goals "The pieces"
    * The **lexer** is an awk define that rewrites program text into one dispatchable goal per line
    * The **stack** is `io.stack.*`
    * Each **operator** is a target
    * The decoded program is run by `mk.kernel.each`, which dispatches each line as an instruction (order and repetition preserved).

Bundled programs include a quine, a palindromic quine, and an (abstracted) factorial.

```bash {.cli_example}
# Run the bundled demo programs:
$ ./demos/underload.mk

# Evaluate a program from stdin:
$ printf '(Hello, world!)S' | ./demos/underload.mk ul.eval

# A quine, which prints its own source:
$ printf '(a(:^)*S):^' | ./demos/underload.mk ul.eval
```

### Underload Demo
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Here are two `compose.mk` implementations of the interpreter: toggle the tabs for the [cmk-lang]({{_}}/cmk/compiler) version (shown first) or its plain `Makefile` twin.  The Makefile version leans only on the [standard library]({{_}}/standard-lib); the cmk-lang version keeps that foundation but shows off more of the language primitives, and reuses the Makefile's shared pieces rather than duplicating them.

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/underload.mk', 'demos/cmk/underload.cmk',
    github=github, default='cmk')}}

A few things the cmk-lang version highlights:

**Single-sourcing / Imports:** we keep things DRY by pulling in shared components that we don't plan to change, i.e. pulling in shared definitions from the Makefile twin. See also the documentation for:

* [`import.def`]({{_}}/macros)
* [`import.target`]({{_}}/macros)

**Triple-Quoted Inlines:** Are a form that CMK supports, with and without interpolation.
See also the documentation [here]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#triple-quote-literals).

**Call Forms:** The `underload` VM reads its program from stdin, so the demos invoke
it with the `[stream]` call-form sugar applied to the `underload` receiver, which lowers
to piping the triple-quoted literal into a recursive call.  See also the documentation
[here]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#callforms).

